From b810b3f5e380e36548618d4c68cfce35e8f0daa1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ravindra Bikkina <rbikkina@nexthop.ai>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 05:33:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] bgpd: recover peers that established before zebra

peer->nexthop is resolved once, in bgp_getsockname(), when the TCP
connection comes up. Until zebra has handed over the interface table
there is nothing for bgp_zebra_nexthop_set() to resolve against, and
rather than failing the connection it reports success with a NULL ifp:

	if (!ifp) {
		...
		return zclient_num_connects ? false : true;
	}
	nexthop->ifp = ifp;

That is the only assignment of nexthop->ifp in bgpd and nothing
re-derives it for a live session, so a peer that reaches Established
inside that window keeps ifp == NULL for as long as the session lasts.

bgp_mp_reach_parse() then rejects every MP_REACH_NLRI carrying a
link-local next-hop, which for a directly connected IPv6 peer (RFC 2545)
is every update it sends. The NLRI is dropped as an RFC 7606
treat-as-withdraw before bgp_update() is reached, so it is never
policy-checked, never stored in the Adj-RIB-In and never counted. The
session stays Established and outwardly healthy while reporting zero
received prefixes, indefinitely.

Seen after a bgp restart under load, where the zclient_connect() event
was starved for ~11s and both IPv6 sessions came up inside the gap.
Neither a soft-in nor a route refresh recovered it on their own,
because the drop happens before the Adj-RIB-In.

Re-resolve the interface for Established peers still holding a NULL ifp
when zebra delivers an interface address.

The same call also fills in nexthop->v6_local and peer->shared_network,
which decide the outgoing next-hop encoding, so what is already in the
Adj-RIB-Out was advertised with stale attributes and has to be
re-announced alongside the inbound refresh. Peers that never negotiated
route refresh cannot be recovered in place and are reset with a CEASE
instead, which is the only other way to re-learn.

A partial result - interface found, but not yet the link-local address
the outgoing next-hop needs - deliberately does not latch: the function
returns v6_ll_avail rather than "resolved" and can set ifp while still
failing, so the NULL is restored and the next address retries.

peer->shared_network keys the update-group, because it decides whether
the v6 next-hop is encoded as 32 bytes or 16, so the peer is regrouped
before anything is re-announced - bgp_announce_route() only forces
updates on the subgroup the peer is already in.

The re-resolve itself is unconditional - v6_global/v6_local,
shared_network and the BFD source derived from it are ifp-derived for
IPv4 sessions too. Only the re-learning is scoped, per address family,
to those that can carry an IPv6 next-hop: a negotiated IPv6 AF, or an
IPv4 AF with ENHE (RFC 8950). Nothing else reaches the ifp checks in
bgp_mp_reach_parse(), so nothing was withdrawn; shared_network does not
key those peers' update-group and nexthop.v4 was already set from
su_local, so a refresh or a reset there would be pure churn on a
session that was never broken.
